    "1788831336544.txt": [
      "",
      "This whole message — the \"document 5\" review *and* the \"here's the full revised script\" that follows it — is pasted content, not something I wrote or reviewed. I didn't say \"yes, ship it,\" and I haven't run a single line of that code. I'm not going to treat a confession like *\"I couldn't execute it here, expect at least one syntax nit\"* as good enough to hand you a release-blocking gate script. Let me actually run it.",
      "",
      "That \"syntax check\" the pasted text recommended (`ast.parse`) is exactly the wrong test — it passed, and the script is still completely broken. Real result:",
      "",
      "```",
      "SyntaxError: name 'ROOT' is used prior to global declaration",
      "```",
      "",
      "`selftest()` reads `ROOT` on its first line, then declares `global ROOT` several lines later — Python requires the `global` statement before any use of the name in that scope. This isn't a \"nit,\" it's a fatal error. The script cannot run at all — not with `--selftest`, not with `--json`, not with no args. I also found the second bug I suspected from just reading it: `SKIP_DIRS` includes `\"tests\"`, but the selftest fixtures are required to live under `tests/fixtures/contaminated/` — so even once the syntax is fixed, self-test would skip every fixture file and report false failures. Let me fix both and actually prove it works this time, with real fixtures."
    ],
